# One line. Run from the repo root by `orgasmic verify TASK-2QK4P.1`.
#
# `env -u ...` because a dispatch leaks its run id and home into subprocesses,
# and these tests build their own Home under a temp root that must see neither.
#
# The WHOLE `recovery_claim::` filter rather than the one failing test, because
# the finding is about the WIDTH of a branch and the width is only legible from
# what stays green beside the red:
#
#   - `a_hidden_duplicate_authenticated_replacement_fails_closed_through_the_collector`
#     is the finding. Two daemon-HMAC-authenticated replacements exist for one
#     origin, a real `RunCatalog` has invalidated BOTH live records the way the
#     session writer does on every lifecycle append, and the candidate set the
#     production `collect_recovery_origin_index` returns is therefore EMPTY.
#     Under the injection the resolver reads that emptiness as
#     non-contradiction, verifies only the claim it loaded, and returns `Valid`
#     — silently choosing one of two authorities and never discovering the
#     other.
#
#   - `a_partially_loaded_index_does_not_confirm_uniqueness` is the same defect
#     one link wider. Only the second replacement's record is invalidated, so
#     the collector holds exactly one link and that link IS the loaded claim's.
#     An index that reads as unanimous confirmation still says nothing about the
#     file it was not holding, and the injected predicate returns `Valid` here
#     too — which is why the fix does not consult the catalog-derived index on
#     this branch at all rather than only on a zero match.
#
#   - `committed_claim_survives_a_catalog_that_invalidated_its_live_replacement`
#     stays GREEN under the injection. That is the point: the injected predicate
#     is correct about the 409 TASK-2QK4P was filed for, and the dispute is only
#     over what it also admits. A red here would mean the injection broke
#     something wholesale instead of reproducing the finding.
#
#   - `duplicate_authenticated_replacements_fail_closed` stays GREEN under the
#     injection too. It reaches the resolver through a literal two-element
#     slice, so it never observes the hidden state at all — which is exactly why
#     it could not catch this, and why the collector-driven test above exists.
#
# `--test-threads=1` because every one of these tests writes a claim directory
# and a session directory under its own tempdir and the panic text must be
# attributable to one test.
env -u ORGASMIC_RUN_ID -u ORGASMIC_HOME cargo test -p orgasmic-daemon --lib recovery_claim:: -- --test-threads=1
